title: '[pallet-revive] Bound per-transaction receipt size'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    The receipt of an Ethereum transaction now holds only the logs of frames that took effect.
    Before, a log stayed in the receipt even when the frame that emitted it reverted, when a
    `utility::batch_all` around the call rolled back, or when the transaction failed while its fee
    was settled. The `ContractEmitted` events of those logs were already rolled back, so the
    receipts served by the ETH RPC did not change, but the block's `logsBloom`, `receiptsRoot`
    and hash committed to the extra logs. They now match the receipts. Tools that check receipts
    against `receiptsRoot` or filter blocks by `logsBloom` see consistent data from this release
    on; blocks produced before it are not rewritten.

    The logs of one Ethereum transaction may now take up at most 2,097,152 bytes (2 MiB) of its
    receipt, counted over the RLP encoding of every log it keeps: address, topics and data, each
    with its RLP headers. The limit counts the logs of every contract the transaction calls and
    applies to `eth_transact`, `eth_substrate_call` and their dry runs, so gas estimation reports
    the same outcome as inclusion. Calls that do not run as an Ethereum transaction, such as
    `Revive::call`, have no receipt and are not affected.

    A log that would take the receipt past the limit fails the call frame that emitted it with
    the new `ReceiptTooLarge` error, like an oversized event does. This covers a `LOG`
    instruction, the `deposit_event` syscall and a log emitted by a precompile, including the
    `Transfer` and `Approval` logs of the pallet-assets ERC-20 precompile. The frame's state
    changes and logs roll back and the calling contract can handle the failure. This applies to
    contracts deployed before the change as well. An Ethereum transaction at the EIP-7825 cap of
    2^24 gas can log at most about 2.09 MB, so only transactions that this chain allowed past
    what Ethereum allows are affected; split such work over several transactions.

    The RLP encoded payload of an `eth_transact` may now be at most 1,245,184 bytes: 1 MiB of
    code, 128 KiB of call data and 64 KiB for the rest of the transaction. A larger payload is
    rejected with `InvalidTransaction::ExhaustsResources` when the transaction is checked, and a
    dry run of it fails with the same error. This also applies to an `eth_transact` to
    `RUNTIME_PALLETS_ADDR`, whose call data is a SCALE encoded runtime call; submit a larger
    runtime call as a native extrinsic. Dev chains with `DebugEnabled` and
    `allow_unlimited_contract_size` can no longer submit EVM init code that takes the payload past
    the limit.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    No storage migration and no configuration change is needed. The new `ReceiptLogsCommitted`
    storage value is only written while an Ethereum transaction or its dry run runs and is empty
    between transactions.

    `Error` has the new variant `ReceiptTooLarge` (index `0x45`). Index `0x44` is left unused so
    that the indexes match later releases. Code that matches on the error exhaustively needs an arm
    for it.

    `PrecompileExt::deposit_event` (`precompiles::Ext` for precompile authors) now returns
    `Result<(), DispatchError>`. It fails with `ReceiptTooLarge` when the log does not fit the
    open receipt, and deposits nothing in that case. The trait is sealed, so no implementation
    changes, but a custom precompile that emits logs through it should propagate the result so
    that the precompile call fails with the error:

    ```rust
    // before
    env.deposit_event(topics, data);
    // after
    env.deposit_event(topics, data)?;
    ```

    Ignoring the result compiles with an unused `Result` warning and does not keep the log: the
    precompile's frame then fails with `ReceiptTooLarge` when it returns.

    The weights shipped with pallet-revive are not regenerated for this release. These benchmarks
    now measure more work: `seal_deposit_event` runs with an Ethereum receipt open, so it includes
    adding the log to the receipt; `on_finalize_per_event` and `on_finalize_per_event_data` put
    their logs into the transaction's receipt; `seal_call`, `seal_call_precompile`,
    `seal_delegate_call`, `seal_instantiate` and `evm_instantiate` include the receipt check when a
    frame returns. Runtimes that use their own `pallet_revive` weights should re-run the
    `pallet_revive` benchmarks. The new storage accesses are to whitelisted storage and add no
    database reads or writes.

    `integrity_test` now sizes the Ethereum block builder from the transaction payload and
    receipt limits instead of from the block weight.

    `pallet-revive-fixtures` gains the `Emitter`, `LogVolume` and `LogVolumeConstructor`
    fixtures. The existing fixtures are unchanged.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
  validate: false
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-revive-uapi
  bump: none
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
